<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hark — TOP-LEVEL app entitlements (the Electron .app bundle).

  Applied by electron-builder to Contents/MacOS/Hark (the Electron host).
  Keep this list MINIMAL: every entitlement widens the threat model and
  privacy-auditor will challenge each one. We ship the fewest that let a
  hardened-runtime Electron app run a JIT (V8) and capture audio.

  Hardened runtime is ON (electron-builder mac.hardenedRuntime: true).
  Hardened runtime is REQUIRED for notarization, and it is also what makes
  the code identity stable enough for TCC to remember the audio-capture grant.

  WHAT WE DELIBERATELY DO NOT INCLUDE (privacy / threat-model reasons):
    * com.apple.security.network.client / .server — harkd is loopback-only
      (127.0.0.1 WebSocket). It never opens an outbound socket. The only
      network path in the product is the explicit, user-invoked Claude API
      call, which goes through the SYSTEM (Electron net), not through a
      raw socket harkd opens, and is gated by user action (CLAUDE.md rule 1).
      If a future feature needs harkd to talk to the network, that requires
      an ADR FIRST (CLAUDE.md rule 6) and only then this entitlement.
    * com.apple.security.app-sandbox — we are NOT App-Store sandboxing v1.
      Sandbox would block the loopback socket handshake, the vault path
      outside the container (~/Documents/vault/hark), and Process Tap APIs.
      Notarization does NOT require the sandbox; only the App Store does.
    * com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation — NOT needed. The
      sidecar (harkd) is signed with the SAME Team ID as the app, so library
      validation passes. We only add this if a third-party UNSIGNED dylib
      ever has to load, which it must not (see the bundling map: harkd is a
      single self-contained Mach-O).
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      V8 (and Electron's renderer) JIT-compile JavaScript. Under hardened
      runtime, generating executable code at runtime is blocked unless one
      of these is present. allow-jit is the narrow, correct one for a JIT.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Electron / Chromium maps writable+executable pages for code generation
      beyond the strict JIT region. Electron's own signing guidance requires
      this for the framework to run under hardened runtime. It is broad-ish
      but is the documented, expected pairing for Electron + hardened runtime.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      Microphone capture. Maps to the TCC Microphone service. Paired with
      NSMicrophoneUsageDescription in Info.plist (extendInfo). Required so
      harkd (child, inheriting this entitlement) can open the mic source.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
